# Setup Agent Stack

Enable automatic skill routing in the current host's global instructions, so the user can describe work without naming each skill. Add a compact managed section that selects through [/skill-issue](../skill-issue/SKILL.md.tmpl) and follows its selected workflows and supporting disciplines.

## Scope

Use after Agent Stack is installed, or to refresh its global routing section. Configure Codex or Claude Code, using the current host unless the user selects another one. Configure both only when requested.

Repository catalog and project-entry maintenance belongs to [/update-router](https://github.com/josuamittels/agent-stack/blob/main/src/skills/update-router/SKILL.md.tmpl), not this skill. Skill installation and global instruction setup are separate operations.

### Boundaries

Modify only the selected host's global instruction file and this skill's own marked section. Preserve existing manual content and permissions. Do not rewrite project instructions, install skills, copy a full routing catalog, alter host settings, or configure another host implicitly. Creating this skill does not run it against the author's global files.

A request to set up global routing authorizes the scoped edit. Preview the concrete target and section before applying it; ask only for an unresolved target, a conflicting manual rule, or a required host permission. Respect existing sandbox approval requirements. Refuse malformed markers and symbolic instruction files instead of guessing what to replace.

## Inputs

Use the current or requested host, its active configuration directory, the host's installed skill catalog, and readable public installation entry for the router. Require the absolute installation root containing its public SKILL.md entry. Bundled dependency snapshots do not establish public installation.

Read [host targets and discovery](references/hosts.md) before selecting a file. If the router prerequisite is missing, report the missing installation before writing. Project-scoped skills can support the current project; they remain unavailable elsewhere unless installed there or globally.

### Default Procedures

When task input is needed from the user, route the question through [/ask-user-question](../ask-user-question/SKILL.md.tmpl) for preparation, delivery, and interpretation. First invoke [/think-harder](../think-harder/SKILL.md.tmpl) when ambiguity, missing information, or competing interpretations may be resolved from context, relevant guidance, evidence, or a reasonable default. Skip [/think-harder](../think-harder/SKILL.md.tmpl) for direct permission or approval gates, explicitly requested interviews, and facts or preferences only the user can supply. Ask only about consequential unresolved items. Continue independent work and preserve approval requirements.

If any discipline is unavailable, perform it directly and report the fallback. Carry unresolved constraints forward and do not invent answers or pretend the dependency ran.

## Workflow

### 1. Identify the active target and skills

Confirm Codex or Claude Code from the active host or explicit request. Resolve its global configuration directory and inspect the existing instruction file, including a Codex override when present. Follow the host reference rather than assuming the base AGENTS.md is loaded.

Confirm the public installed router is this stack's router. Inspect its actual entry, its scope, and any host invocation names or plugin namespaces. Do not search unrelated personal files or treat a skill with the same name as equivalent. Check existing manual routing for direct conflicts; preserve it and resolve only material conflicts through the default question procedure.

### 2. Preview the managed section

Read [the global routing template](templates/global-routing.md) for the exact section body. Its skill links identify capabilities for the reader; future routing resolves installed names through the current host rather than fetching remote documentation or pinning one project path.

Run [the setup helper](scripts/setup.mjs) without --apply. It validates installed entries and marker boundaries, selects the active target, and returns its proposed block and before_sha256 digest without writing.

From the source checkout:

```sh
# Codex
node src/skills/setup-agent-stack/scripts/setup.mjs --host codex --skills-dir /absolute/installed/skills

# Claude Code
node src/skills/setup-agent-stack/scripts/setup.mjs --host claude-code --skills-dir /absolute/installed/skills
```

In an installed package, resolve the helper from this skill's bundled scripts instead of the source checkout. Use --config-dir only for an explicitly selected or host-established custom profile. The helper otherwise honors CODEX_HOME or CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, then the host's usual home directory. Pass paths explicitly on each call; do not assume shell variables persist.

Show the resolved target, whether it is global, and the routing section. Continue within the user's existing authorization. An unchanged preview still needs the apply-mode verification below; it should not rewrite the file.

### 3. Apply without replacing manual instructions

Rerun the same helper with --apply --expected-sha256 and the digest returned by the preview, using the literal value missing for a nonexistent target. It creates the file or appends when no owned markers exist. On rerun it replaces exactly one balanced agent-stack:global-routing block, preserving all bytes before and after it. It creates a uniquely named backup before changing an existing file and preserves its mode. Unchanged files receive no write or backup.

If the file or active target changes after preview, preview again and reassess the new content. Stop on malformed markers, an instruction-file symlink, a manual conflict, or a denied write. Report the exact remaining action; do not silently change to project scope or another host. Backups belong beside the user's target file, never inside installed packages.

### 4. Verify and record the result

Read the complete resulting file. Confirm one managed section, the routing rules, preserved surrounding content, and the helper's idempotence result. Keep the helper's apply JSON as machine-readable evidence in temporary working storage, with output_files.instructions pointing to the full global instruction file. Do not persist personal instruction contents or evaluation records inside installed resources.

For the objective invocation record, pass evidence.setup as the helper's complete apply result. Resolve the host constraint with value codex or claude-code and source request or skill_default. Preserve the original user request. Preview-only, blocked, and partial attempts are not completed setup and do not get a success verdict. For both requested hosts, finish and check each target separately and report partial completion accurately.

Tell the user to start a new session to load global instructions. For Claude Code, inspect /memory in that session; for Codex, ask for the loaded instruction sources. File checks establish the persisted configuration, not that a future agent obeyed it.

### 5. Validate implementation

When invoked as a helper, return to the owning workflow without separate evaluation. For a skill with meaningful, deterministic, task-specific checks, invoke [/spawn-evals](../spawn-evals/SKILL.md.tmpl) once with a structured invocation record containing the original request, resolved constraints and their source, complete UTF-8 outputs, and machine-readable evidence. Its objective package in `src/evals/setup-agent-stack/` defines only those checks. Report PASS, FAIL, or NOT PASSED — UNVERIFIED with the failing or unavailable verification steps. Do not use model judges, sub-agents, qualitative assessments, weighted scores, or synthetic scenarios. If no package exists for an objectively checkable skill, report NOT PASSED — UNVERIFIED; do not create one during an ordinary skill invocation. If no meaningful objective check is possible for this skill, omit this entire validation section and all [/spawn-evals](../spawn-evals/SKILL.md.tmpl) references from the skill.

## Output

Return the selected host, absolute target, created/appended/updated/unchanged status, and any backup path. Explain that tasks route through the installed router, which selects the appropriate workflows and supporting disciplines. State the installation scope, reload step, objective result for a completed invocation, and any missing prerequisites or unresolved conflicts. Do not claim that setup installed skills or verified future agent behavior.
